WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.833
Netflix is two machines
wearing one coat.

2
00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:06.049
Good news, everyone, today
we take both of them apart!

3
00:00:06.211 --> 00:00:07.628
It sounds simple:

4
00:00:07.721 --> 00:00:10.136
pick a movie, press play, watch.

5
00:00:10.298 --> 00:00:13.351
But behind that one
click hide some truly

6
00:00:13.468 --> 00:00:15.139
enormous problems.

7
00:00:15.337 --> 00:00:18.274
First, where do we
keep all these movies?

8
00:00:18.471 --> 00:00:21.745
A single film can take several
gigabytes, and there are

9
00:00:21.815 --> 00:00:22.860
thousands of them.

10
00:00:22.976 --> 00:00:26.610
So the originals live
in big object storage.

11
00:00:26.773 --> 00:00:30.151
We do not fling one colossal
file at your television.

12
00:00:30.244 --> 00:00:31.835
That would be idiotic!

13
00:00:32.148 --> 00:00:33.251
No no no.

14
00:00:33.448 --> 00:00:36.629
My transcoding apparatus
grinds every movie into

15
00:00:36.722 --> 00:00:37.588
many versions.

16
00:00:37.628 --> 00:00:39.195
There's 4K, 1080p,

17
00:00:39.253 --> 00:00:43.015
720p, and 480p, different bitrates,

18
00:00:43.154 --> 00:00:44.617
different codecs.

19
00:00:44.768 --> 00:00:45.390
Why?

20
00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:48.727
Because fiber and a 4K
television get a glorious

21
00:00:48.774 --> 00:00:51.931
stream, while the poor
soul on wobbly mobile data

22
00:00:52.047 --> 00:00:53.382
gets a smaller one.

23
00:00:53.498 --> 00:00:56.087
We call it adaptive bitrate streaming.

24
00:00:56.203 --> 00:00:57.162
I invented it!

25
00:00:57.202 --> 00:00:58.202
Possibly.

26
00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:00.151
Now, delivery!

27
00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:03.553
If Europe streamed from one
American data center, every

28
00:01:03.645 --> 00:01:05.840
bit would schlep across an ocean.

29
00:01:06.002 --> 00:01:08.557
Slow and monstrously expensive.

30
00:01:08.696 --> 00:01:12.527
So we use a CDN, which puts
copies on servers near the

31
00:01:12.574 --> 00:01:13.184
viewers.

32
00:01:13.224 --> 00:01:14.582
Watching from Paris?

33
00:01:14.698 --> 00:01:17.345
Then your movie comes from Paris!

34
00:01:17.543 --> 00:01:21.246
Netflix even built its
own, called Open Connect.

35
00:01:21.478 --> 00:01:23.777
Now the viewer presses play.

36
00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:27.074
The backend does the
boring paperwork, like

37
00:01:27.132 --> 00:01:30.430
authentication, subscriptions,
and choosing which

38
00:01:30.499 --> 00:01:31.637
file you deserve.

39
00:01:31.718 --> 00:01:35.468
But the video never squeezes
through those servers.

40
00:01:35.631 --> 00:01:37.558
What a bottleneck!

41
00:01:37.674 --> 00:01:41.552
The app takes its instructions
and streams straight from the CDN.

42
00:01:41.668 --> 00:01:45.117
But your internet speed flails
about, so the player keeps

43
00:01:45.256 --> 00:01:46.063
measuring it.

44
00:01:46.103 --> 00:01:48.634
Speed drops, you get lower quality.

45
00:01:48.774 --> 00:01:51.189
It recovers, back up you go!

46
00:01:51.351 --> 00:01:54.486
And since videos come in
little chunks, we swap

47
00:01:54.579 --> 00:01:57.760
quality mid-stream without you noticing.

48
00:01:58.027 --> 00:02:01.336
Now a new season drops,
and millions press

49
00:02:01.406 --> 00:02:02.474
play at once.

50
00:02:02.636 --> 00:02:04.146
One server for all that?

51
00:02:04.238 --> 00:02:05.446
Preposterous!

52
00:02:05.562 --> 00:02:08.581
So the system splits
into many services, for

53
00:02:08.697 --> 00:02:12.040
users, billing, playback,
and search, each

54
00:02:12.273 --> 00:02:13.654
scaling alone.

55
00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:17.056
The databases are
distributed and replicated.

56
00:02:17.207 --> 00:02:19.877
Losing your watch
history is annoying, but

57
00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:22.246
losing playback is catastrophic!

58
00:02:22.443 --> 00:02:23.627
Simplified, then:

59
00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:25.926
Netflix is two machines.

60
00:02:26.124 --> 00:02:29.328
One runs the application,
with users, payments,

61
00:02:29.409 --> 00:02:30.466
and metadata.

62
00:02:30.535 --> 00:02:32.463
The other hauls the video.

63
00:02:32.602 --> 00:02:35.934
Storage prepares it,
the CDN spreads it, and

64
00:02:36.004 --> 00:02:38.001
adaptive streaming picks the quality.

65
00:02:38.082 --> 00:02:41.565
You see, the hard part
was never the play button.

66
00:02:41.646 --> 00:02:44.607
It's delivering billions
of chunks to every last

67
00:02:44.665 --> 00:02:48.311
person on this miserable,
wonderful planet.

68
00:02:48.624 --> 00:02:49.924
Good night, everyone!

69
00:02:50.004 --> 00:02:52.790
Comment NETFLIX and I'll
send you the schematic.
